"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][9]={"school_physics_en:M9-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M9-L1 – Revisiting charge, current and voltage<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Electrical networks and power<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A torch can shine for minutes without steadily running out of\nelectrons in the filament. What circulates in the circuit, and where\ndoes the energy of the light come from?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Electric charge<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a conserved quantity measured in coulombs\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nElectrons move through metal conductors. <strong>Conventional current\ndirection<\/strong> is defined from positive to negative, opposite to the\ndrift of the electrons. <strong>Current<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the charge that crosses a selected section per time interval:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=\\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm A=1\\,\\mathrm{C/s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><strong>Voltage<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbetween two points is the change in energy per unit of transported\ncharge,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=\\Delta E/\\Delta Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts unit is the volt:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm V=1\\,\\mathrm{J/C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCurrent is not an amount of energy, and voltage is not a substance used\nup in a load. The source maintains a potential difference and transfers\nenergy via the circuit; a device subsequently transfers energy, for\nexample by emitting light or heating the surroundings.<\/p>\n<p>For a <strong>two-terminal component<\/strong>, distinguish the\ncurrent\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>through<\/strong> the component from the voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>across<\/strong> it. Connect an ammeter in series and a voltmeter\nin parallel with the component. In a steady unbranched circuit the\ncurrent is the same along the entire path. <strong>Original teaching\ndiagram:<\/strong> A two-terminal resistor with a conventional current\narrow, voltage arrow and separate labels for both quantities.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_051_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A resistor with a conventional current arrow through it and a separate voltage arrow across its terminals.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A resistor with a conventional current\narrow through it and a separate voltage arrow across its\nterminals.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Current describes through-flow; voltage compares two\nterminals.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A device carries\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0.20\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=30\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe charge passing a cross-section is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta Q=I\\Delta t=0.20\\cdot30=6.0\\,\\mathrm C.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If the voltage across the device is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=3.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe model gives an energy transfer of<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E=U\\Delta Q=3.0\\cdot6.0=18\\,\\mathrm J.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof charge has not itself been converted into light.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe comparison:<\/strong> First draw a supply, resistor and\nmeasurement points on paper. Physical measurements require an approved,\ncurrent-limited, safely isolated extra-low-voltage classroom supply with\nteacher supervision. Switch off before reconnecting anything. Never use\nmains sockets, exposed live conductors or high-power battery packs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">State the units of charge, current and voltage.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What charge passes a section at 0.50 A in eight seconds?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How much energy is transferred at 6 V per 2 C of charge?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can a load receive energy without permanently using up\ncharge?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Coulomb (C), ampere (A) and volt (V).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Q = 0.50 A × 8 s = 4.0 C.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">E = UQ = 6 V × 2 C = 12 J.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Charge carriers continue around the circuit, while the source and\ndevice transfer energy without destroying charge.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The simple model describes steady direct current. Alternating-current\ncircuits and fast switching require a time-dependent treatment. Electron\ndrift, conventional current direction and energy transfer must not be\nconfused.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lower-secondary\ncurriculum: current, voltage, resistance, circuits, power and energy ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/elektrik\">DGUV\nSafe School – Electricity experiments<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Safe extra-low-voltage\nsupplies and safety measures for pupils · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M9-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M9-L2 – Electrical resistance and Ohm’s law<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Electrical networks and power<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why does doubling the voltage often double the current through a\nfixed technical resistor, but not through an incandescent lamp?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>At a measured operating point of a two-terminal device,\n<strong>electrical resistance<\/strong> is defined by<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>U<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>I<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\frac UI,\\qquad 1\\,\\Omega=1\\,\\mathrm{V/A},\\quad I\\ne0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A component is <strong>ohmic<\/strong> when voltage and current are\nproportional at constant temperature:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=RI<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent of the operating point. A straight line through the origin\non a plot of <strong>voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhorizontally and current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvertically<\/strong> has slope\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/U=1/R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen voltage is plotted against current, the slope is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninstead. Axis choice must be explicit.<\/p>\n<p>A resistor limits current at a given voltage and can transfer\nelectrical energy into internal energy. Metal conductors may warm up;\ntheir resistance can then change with temperature and their measured\ncharacteristic need not be exactly straight. <strong>Original teaching\ndiagram:<\/strong> Model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>–<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncharacteristic of an ohmic resistor with three sample points and a slope\ntriangle. These are designed example values, not laboratory\nmeasurements.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_052_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"An I-versus-U graph: 2, 4 and 6 V correspond to 0.10, 0.20 and 0.30 A; U/I is 20 ohms.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">An I-versus-U graph: 2, 4 and 6 V\ncorrespond to 0.10, 0.20 and 0.30 A; U/I is 20 ohms.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Ohmic behaviour means constant U/I within the chosen thermal\nrange.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>Take a model component with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=2.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand currents\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0.10\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.30\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\frac{2.0\\,\\mathrm V}{0.10\\,\\mathrm A}=20\\,\\Omega.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The other two ratios also equal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese proportional data are consistent with ohmic behaviour over the\ntested range. A single data point cannot establish this pattern.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Supervised low-voltage experiment:<\/strong> Use an approved\nclassroom supply, a suitably rated technical resistor and suitable\nmeters. Put the ammeter in series and the voltmeter across the resistor.\nIncrease the voltage only within the equipment ratings and switch off\nbefore changing connections. As a paper-based alternative, calculate\nratios from the supplied table.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the SI-derived unit of resistance?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find R for U = 9 V and I = 0.30 A.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does a straight line through the origin in an I-versus-U graph\nmean?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is an operating incandescent lamp often non-ohmic?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The ohm (Ω), equal to volt per ampere.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">R = 9 V / 0.30 A = 30 Ω.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Current is proportional to voltage; the slope equals 1/R.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Its filament gets hotter at larger current and its resistance\nincreases.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=U/I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndefines a ratio at one operating point; Ohm’s law additionally requires\nthe ratio to stay constant across operating points in the chosen range.\nThermal conditions and component ratings matter.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/ohmsches-gesetz-kennlinien/grundwissen/ohmsches-gesetz\">LEIFIphysik\n– Ohm’s law<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Proportional U–I behaviour only for ohmic elements;\nresistance ratio U/I · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/ohmsches-gesetz-kennlinien/grundwissen/kennlinien-von-widerstaenden\">LEIFIphysik\n– Characteristics of resistors<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Linear and nonlinear U–I\ncharacteristics and temperature dependence · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lower-secondary\ncurriculum: current, voltage, resistance, circuits, power and energy ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M9-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M9-L3 – Series and parallel circuits<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Electrical networks and power<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Two resistors are connected first one after the other and then side\nby side to the same source. Which arrangement draws more current?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>In a <strong>series circuit<\/strong>, both resistors share a single\ncurrent path. The current is equal through both components, and the\nsource voltage is distributed:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=I_1=I_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=U_1+U_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor ohmic resistors,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{\\mathrm{series}}=R_1+R_2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In a <strong>parallel circuit<\/strong>, both components connect\nbetween the same two nodes. Consequently,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=U_1=U_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile currents add:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=I_1+I_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor ohmic resistors,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1{R_{\\mathrm{parallel}}}=\\frac1{R_1}+\\frac1{R_2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For positive resistances, the parallel equivalent is smaller than\neither component. The rules assume ideal wires and steady operation; the\nsource must be capable of supplying the combined current.\n<strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> The same two labelled\nresistors in series and parallel, with arrows for total and branch\ncurrent. In practical experiments ammeters belong in the appropriate\ncurrent path, whereas voltmeters are connected across components.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_053_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two resistors in a single series path on the left; two parallel branches between shared nodes on the right.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two resistors in a single series path on\nthe left; two parallel branches between shared nodes on the\nright.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Current is shared in series; voltage is shared in parallel under\nstated model assumptions.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>An ideal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsupply drives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nohmic resistors. In <strong>series<\/strong>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>60<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{total}=60\\,\\Omega,\\quad I=6.0/60=0.10\\,\\mathrm A,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_1=2.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_2=4.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn <strong>parallel<\/strong>, both resistors have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacross them:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_1=0.30\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_2=0.15\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe total is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.45<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.45\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncorresponding to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>13.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{total}=13.3\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(rounded).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Classroom experiment:<\/strong> Compare two suitably rated\nresistors using a supervised current-limited extra-low-voltage supply.\nSwitch off before changing the layout. Do not substitute incandescent\nlamps for fixed resistors when checking the equations quantitatively.\nPredict currents first and check ratings for both resistors and the\nsupply.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What stays equal across two series resistors?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the total resistance of 10 Ω and 30 Ω in series.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the total resistance of two 40-Ω resistors in parallel.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the parallel arrangement usually draw more current from an\nideal voltage source?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The current through both components.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Rtotal = 10 Ω + 30 Ω = 40 Ω.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">1/Rtotal = 1/40 + 1/40 = 1/20, giving Rtotal = 20 Ω.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The equivalent resistance is lower, so I = U/Rtotal is greater.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Real power supplies have internal resistance and current limits.\nFixed-resistance formulas must not be applied blindly to nonlinear\ndevices. Parallel components share voltage, not necessarily current.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/komplexere-schaltkreise/grundwissen/reihenschaltung-von-widerstaenden\">LEIFIphysik\n– Resistors in series<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Equivalent resistance and voltage splitting\nin series · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/komplexere-schaltkreise/grundwissen/parallelschaltung-von-widerstaenden\">LEIFIphysik\n– Resistors in parallel<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Equivalent resistance and branch currents\nin parallel · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lower-secondary\ncurriculum: current, voltage, resistance, circuits, power and energy ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M9-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M9-L4 – Kirchhoff’s rules at school level<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Electrical networks and power<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>At a junction one current splits into two branches; the branches join\nagain later. Why can their currents not take arbitrary values?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>junction rule<\/strong> follows from conservation of\nelectric charge. In a steady circuit the total current flowing\n<strong>into<\/strong> a node equals the total flowing\n<strong>out<\/strong>. For one incoming and two outgoing currents:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\mathrm{in}=I_1+I_2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The <strong>loop rule<\/strong> describes potential changes around a\nclosed path. With a consistently chosen direction, all\n<strong>signed<\/strong> voltage changes add to zero. For one ideal\nsource and two series loads, the classroom form is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm{source}=U_1+U_2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>These rules are not competing mnemonics: they express charge and\nenergy conservation in the steady or quasistatic network model. Signs\nmatter. Going around the loop, a voltage rise across an ideal source is\nassigned the opposite sign from a voltage drop across a load.\n<strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> A three-current junction on\nthe left and a closed loop with a source and two resistors on the\nright.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_054_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"An incoming 0.50 A splits into 0.20 A and 0.30 A, with a separate loop showing 9 = 3 + 6 volts.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">An incoming 0.50 A splits into 0.20 A and\n0.30 A, with a separate loop showing 9 = 3 + 6 volts.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Junction and loop rules illustrate conservation of charge and\nenergy.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>An incoming current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\mathrm{in}=0.50\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsplits into\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_1=0.20\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe junction rule gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_2=0.50-0.20=0.30\\,\\mathrm A.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In a <strong>separate<\/strong> ideal circuit, two series loads are\nsupplied by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm{source}=9.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe voltage across the first is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_1=3.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nleaving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_2=6.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the second. These examples are independent; they do not define one\ncombined network.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Paper exercise:<\/strong> Draw a junction, choose arrow\ndirections and check current balance. Next draw one loop and mark the\nvoltage rise and voltage drops. Any physical test should use supervised,\nisolated extra-low voltage, suitable resistors and a source that is\nswitched off before rewiring.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">State the junction rule in one sentence.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the second branch current if 0.80 A enters and 0.35 A leaves\nvia the first branch.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An ideal 12-V supply feeds two series loads; U1 = 5 V. Find U2.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is choosing a loop direction important?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For steady conditions, total current into a node equals total\ncurrent out.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">I2 = 0.80 A − 0.35 A = 0.45 A.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">U2 = 12 V − 5 V = 7 V.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The choice fixes the signs of voltage changes but does not change\nthe physical result.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The simple loop rule here assumes a quasistatic circuit without\nchanging magnetic flux threading the loop. A negative solved current\nmeans that the actual current direction is opposite to the chosen\narrow.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/komplexere-schaltkreise/grundwissen/kirchhoffsche-gesetze\">LEIFIphysik\n– Kirchhoff’s circuit rules<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Junction rule from charge\nconservation; loop rule from energy account · Last accessed: 2026-11-21\n· Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lower-secondary\ncurriculum: current, voltage, resistance, circuits, power and energy ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M9-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M9-L5 – Electrical power and electrical energy<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Electrical networks and power<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A small motor runs briefly while an LED shines for many hours. Which\nquantity measures the rate of energy transfer, and which measures the\ntotal transferred energy?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Electrical power<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis energy transferred per unit time. For a DC two-terminal device with\nconstant current and voltage,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=UI,\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm W=1\\,\\mathrm{J/s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The <strong>energy<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntransferred during time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=Pt=UIt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSome school contexts also call this electrical work\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_\\mathrm{el}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEnergy and power must be kept separate:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{kW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a unit of power, whereas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{kWh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a unit of energy. One kilowatt-hour is one kilowatt sustained for one\nhour:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mn>3600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.6<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{kWh}=1000\\,\\mathrm W\\times3600\\,\\mathrm s=3.6\\times10^6\\,\\mathrm J.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For ohmic resistors,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=I^2R=U^2/R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan also be used. For a nonlinear component,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=UI<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstill applies at the particular operating point even when resistance\nvaries. <strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> An energy input to a\ndevice, its power label and a time axis; the area model is explicitly\nlimited to constant power.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_055_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A device at 6 V and 0.50 A; a rectangular power-time area shows 3 W for 600 seconds equals 1800 J.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A device at 6 V and 0.50 A; a rectangular\npower-time area shows 3 W for 600 seconds equals 1800 J.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>For constant power, transferred energy equals power multiplied by\nduration.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A supervised model device operates at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=6.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0.50\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=10\\,\\mathrm{min}=600\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1800<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=6.0\\times0.50=3.0\\,\\mathrm W,\\qquad E=3.0\\times600=1800\\,\\mathrm J.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.0005<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.0005\\,\\mathrm{kWh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe 3.0 W describes the power, while 1800 J is the total energy\ntransferred during the interval.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe data exercise:<\/strong> Compare three supplied\ncombinations of voltage, current and duration on paper. Practical\nmeasurements require supervised, isolated current-limited extra-low\nvoltage, suitable resistors and meters. Do not measure power at\nhousehold outlets.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which unit describes power and which describes energy?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate P at 12 V and 0.25 A.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A 5-W model device operates for 120 s. How much energy is\ntransferred?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Convert 0.25 kWh into joules.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Watt (W) measures power; joule (J) or kWh measures energy.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">P = 12 V × 0.25 A = 3 W.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">E = 5 W × 120 s = 600 J.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">0.25 × 3.6 MJ = 0.9 MJ = 900,000 J.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>For time-varying power, energy must be summed across intervals or\nintegrated. An appliance’s electrical input power is not necessarily its\nuseful mechanical or light output power.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L6<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/elektrische-energie/grundwissen/elektrische-leistung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Electrical power<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · P = UI and additional forms in terms of\nresistance · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/elektrische-energie/grundwissen/elektrische-energie\">LEIFIphysik\n– Electrical energy<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · E = P t and conversion 1 kWh = 3.6 MJ · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lower-secondary\ncurriculum: current, voltage, resistance, circuits, power and energy ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M9-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M9-L6 – Characteristics, measurement errors and real components<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Electrical networks and power<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>In an incandescent lamp, current does not always increase in direct\nproportion to voltage. Is that a measurement error or a property of the\nheated filament?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>characteristic curve<\/strong> shows how current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges with voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor an ohmic resistor kept at a stable temperature, a plot of current\nagainst voltage is a straight line through the origin. In a filament\nlamp, resistance typically rises with temperature, so current increases\n<strong>less than proportionally<\/strong> at higher applied voltage.\nThis reproducible behaviour is a component property, not an error that\nshould be erased by forcing a straight-line fit.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Measurement problems<\/strong> include random scatter (small\nreading fluctuations), systematic errors (for example a zero offset or\nincorrect range) and model mismatch (for example assuming constant\ntemperature). Draw axes, units and all data points. At one operating\npoint the resistance ratio is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(U)=U/I(U)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich may vary across the curve. The slope\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta I/\\Delta U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not itself resistance for this axis choice.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> A straight ohmic model\nline and a downward-flattening filament-lamp model curve share the same\naxes. An isolated point is marked for possible rechecking, not silently\ndeleted.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_056_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Voltage horizontally, current vertically: a straight ohmic line and flattening model filament curve.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Voltage horizontally, current vertically:\na straight ohmic line and flattening model filament curve.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>A curved characteristic may be a genuine feature of the\ndevice.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose a model filament has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_1=2.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_1=0.20\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_2=6.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_2=0.30\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.20<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.30<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_1=2.0/0.20=10\\,\\Omega,\\qquad R_2=6.0/0.30=20\\,\\Omega.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The rising ratio suggests temperature-dependent or other device\nbehaviour. These numbers are deliberately constructed teaching values.\nRepeating the measurement under controlled conditions helps identify\nsystematic errors and possible outliers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Data exercise without a power supply:<\/strong> Plot the two\nfictional model points from the worked example and an explicitly\ninvented third point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_3=4.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.26<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_3=0.26\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin an I-versus-U diagram. Label axes in V and A, then explain why a\ncurved model characteristic is plausible from the three points. If a\nreal experiment is conducted, use supervised approved low-voltage\nequipment; filament lamps may become hot and must not be touched\nimmediately after use.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does an ohmic resistor appear on an I-against-U plot?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find R1 and R2 in the worked example.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give one possible systematic and one random measurement error.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must a curved characteristic not automatically be rejected as\nwrong?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A line through the origin at approximately constant\ntemperature.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">R1 = 10 Ω, R2 = 20 Ω.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Systematic: meter zero offset. Random: small reading\nfluctuations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A nonlinear characteristic can reflect repeatable temperature or\noperating-point behaviour of the device.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Not all nonlinear behaviour comes from heating; diodes have different\nphysical mechanisms. The curves shown here are conceptual and are not\ncopied measured data.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L7<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/ohmsches-gesetz-kennlinien/grundwissen/kennlinien-von-widerstaenden\">LEIFIphysik\n– Characteristics of resistors<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Linear and nonlinear U–I\ncharacteristics and temperature dependence · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/elektrizitaetslehre/ohmsches-gesetz-kennlinien/grundwissen/ohmsches-gesetz\">LEIFIphysik\n– Ohm’s law<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Proportional U–I behaviour only for ohmic elements;\nresistance ratio U/I · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lower-secondary\ncurriculum: current, voltage, resistance, circuits, power and energy ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M9-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M9-L7 – Electrical protection and safe use<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Electrical networks and power<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>An appliance has a metal case and a cable with several conductors.\nWhy is simply switching off its lamp not enough to prove electrical\nsafety?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>Real electrical installations use <strong>several coordinated\nsafeguards<\/strong>. <strong>Protective earth (PE)<\/strong> connects\ndesignated touchable conductive parts to a protective system. For\ncertain faults it provides a route that helps protective devices\ndisconnect the supply; it is <strong>not a normal working-current\nconductor<\/strong>. Whether PE is required depends on the appliance\nprotection class.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>fuse or miniature circuit breaker<\/strong> disconnects\nexcessive current, primarily protecting cables and installations from\noverheating and fire. A <strong>residual-current device (RCD)<\/strong>\ncompares outward and returning currents. A sufficient imbalance causes\nit to trip and can provide additional protection against fault current.\nIt does not replace proper wiring or overcurrent protection, nor does it\nprotect against every possible kind of contact.<\/p>\n<p>This is a <strong>functional teaching diagram, not a wiring or\ninstallation guide<\/strong>. Wire colours and consumer-unit internals\nare deliberately excluded from practical activities. The safe response\nto damaged electrical equipment is to keep clear, not attempt repairs,\nand inform a responsible adult or qualified electrician.\n<strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> Three separate pictogram\nboxes marked PE, overcurrent and RCD, each identifying its distinct\nprotective role without a usable mains-wiring schematic.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_057_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three distinct safety principles: PE for exposed conductive parts, overcurrent protection, and an RCD for current imbalance.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three distinct safety principles: PE for\nexposed conductive parts, overcurrent protection, and an RCD for current\nimbalance.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Protective earth, overcurrent protection and an RCD solve\ndifferent safety problems.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A model circuit uses a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nresistor at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIdeal current is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=U/R=0.50\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis gives <strong>no basis<\/strong> for selecting protection for a\nhousehold socket circuit. In installations, conductor size, supply\narrangement, fault conditions, disconnection time and relevant rules are\nassessed by qualified specialists. Neither this calculation nor a simple\nDC circuit establishes whether an RCD will provide sufficient\nprotection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Observation only:<\/strong> Match the descriptions “overloaded\ncable”, “fault current to earth” and “touchable metal enclosure” to\nappropriate protection principles. Classroom work must use approved\nsafely isolated extra-low-voltage equipment under responsible\nsupervision. Do not open sockets, bypass fuses, disconnect protective\nearth, or dismantle or rewire RCDs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the main role of a circuit breaker protecting a cable?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does an RCD compare in principle?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is protective earth meant to carry normal operating current?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why cannot a 6-V resistor calculation determine protection for a\nmains installation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It disconnects excessive current to protect wiring from overheating\nand fire.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The total current flowing out and returning on the active\nconductors; a difference can indicate fault current.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Protective earth is part of the fault-protection system, not a\nnormal load-current path.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Because it omits the supply system, fault currents, disconnection\nconditions, cable sizing, regulations and expert assessment.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>An RCD is not universal protection from electric shock, especially\nnot for every contact between two active conductors. Protective devices\nmust be correctly selected, coordinated and installed by professionals.\nIf equipment appears faulty, keep clear and inform a responsible\nperson.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The next module begins with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L1 – Electromagnets and electric\nmotors<\/a> and goes on to electromagnetic induction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/elektrik\">DGUV\nSafe School – Electricity experiments<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Safe extra-low-voltage\nsupplies and safety measures for pupils · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/ausstattung-und-geraete/elektrische-installation\">DGUV\nSafe School – Electrical installations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Safety of school experiment\noutlets and RCD protection · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\nEducation Server – Physics: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lower-secondary\ncurriculum: current, voltage, resistance, circuits, power and energy ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
